     1          % =========================================================================
     2          % CHATLIB - the chat wire format, in ONE place, separately compiled.
     3          %
     4          % WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not tidiness.
     5          %
     6          % 1. THE PROTOCOL WAS DECLARED TWICE. The client names 24 message kinds and
     7          %    the server 35, with TWENTY in both. They agree today only because
     8          %    somebody keeps checking; nothing makes them agree. A kind that drifts
     9          %    does not fail loudly - an unhandled kind is SILENT, which is the worst
    10          %    failure this product has. Declared here and IMPORTed by both, a mismatch
    11          %    becomes a LINK error, and LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED already runs in every
    12          %    link we do.
    13          %
    14          % 2. IT MAKES SMALL TEST PROGRAMS POSSIBLE, which is the bigger prize. The
    15          %    whole client is 6400 lines and takes about TWENTY MINUTES to compile, so
    16          %    every one-line change has been tested by building the entire product and
    17          %    driving two live machines. A program that links this library and checks
    18          %    the codec builds in EIGHT SECONDS - measured with SPLITTEST - and needs
    19          %    no XMSG, no server and nobody typing. See CHATTEST.PLNC.
    20          %
    21          % THE WIRE SHAPE, taken from the client's buildOwn and the server's
    22          % buildFromSlot, which independently built the same thing:
    23          %
    24          %   byte 0            kind
    25          %   byte 1            name length
    26          %   bytes 2..         the name
    27          %   next two bytes    text length, HIGH byte then LOW
    28          %   then              the text
    29          %
    30          % The length is two bytes and the high one has always been zero in practice,
    31          % because a message never approaches 256 characters. It is written and read as
    32          % two bytes anyway - the format says two, and a reader that assumed one would
    33          % be wrong the first time somebody sent a long line.
    34          %
    35          % NAMES ARE UNIQUE IN SEVEN CHARACTERS ACROSS A BRF BOUNDARY, so everything
    36          % exported here is six or seven long and distinct within that. cmEnc, cmKind,
    37          % cmNmLen, cmTxAt, cmTxLen.
    38          % =========================================================================
    39          % AN ADDRESS ON THIS MACHINE IS 16 BITS. THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY HERE.
    40          %
    41          % ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE INTEGER4 draws
    42          %     *** WARNING - ILLEGAL DATA-ELEMENT TO BE CONVERTED
    43          % and so does forcing to any 32-bit alias of INTEGER4. The reason is a width
    44          % mismatch, not the name: the product's own working calls force to
    45          % XMUSERADDRESS, and XMP-B02:IMPT declares
    46          %
    47          %     TYPE XMUSERADDRESS = INTEGER          % NB: NEW MC68000 compiler!!
    48          %
    49          % INTEGER - SIXTEEN BITS. The line above it in that file reads
    50          %
    51          %     %        modify XMUSERADDRESS = INTEGER4                      %
    52          %
    53          % which is a COMMENT telling you to widen it FOR THE MC68000. Reading that
    54          % commented instruction as the declaration is what produced a 32-bit force, a
    55          % warning on every ADDR line, and two meaningless numbers that reproduced
    56          % perfectly across builds because the adjacent word was stable.
    57          %
    58          % This library deliberately does not include XMP-B02:DEFS, because it links into
    59          % the test program which has no XMSG at all, so it declares its own of the same
    60          % width. TYPE outside a MODULE is a compiler-level thing, globally available and
    61          % not linked (manual 8.7 p.223), so it costs the test build nothing.
    62          TYPE xrAddr = INTEGER
    63          MODULE chatlib
    64          
    65              EXPORT cmEnc
    66              EXPORT cmEncAt
    67              EXPORT cmEncTo
    68              EXPORT trHLen
    69              EXPORT trOrig
    70              EXPORT trHops
    71              EXPORT trId
    72              EXPORT trStrip
    73              EXPORT xrClamp
    74              EXPORT xrAddrOf
    75              EXPORT trEnc
    76              EXPORT cmKind
    77              EXPORT cmNmLen
    78              EXPORT cmTxAt
    79              EXPORT cmTxLen
    80              EXPORT cmLenN
    81              EXPORT cmSane
    82              EXPORT hsSec
    83              EXPORT hsMin
    84              EXPORT hsHour
    85              EXPORT hsDay
    86              EXPORT hsMon
    87              EXPORT hsYear
    88          
    89              % ---- THE MESSAGE KINDS ----------------------------------------------
    90              %
    91              % ALL TWENTY that the client and the server BOTH declared. They agreed
    92              % only because somebody kept checking; nothing made them agree, and an
    93              % unhandled kind is SILENT - the worst failure this product has. Declared
    94              % here and IMPORTed by both, a drift becomes a LINK error instead, and
    95              % LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED already runs in every link.
    96              %
    97              % THE NUMBERS ARE THE WIRE AND MAY NEVER BE RENUMBERED. A machine running
    98              % an older build is on the other end of the trunk and will read whatever
    99              % it is sent. CHATTST asserts every one of these values for that reason -
   100              % a test that only checks a round trip would happily agree with a renumber.
   101              %
   102              % TWO NAMES CHANGED, and only because they could not cross a BRF boundary:
   103              % names are unique in SEVEN characters there, so kRename/kRenamed both
   104              % read KRENAME and kDirect/kDirected both read KDIRECT. The SECOND of each
   105              % pair was renamed - kRenamed became kRenOk, kDirected became kDirGot -
   106              % which is two renames rather than four.
   107              EXPORT kJoin
   108              EXPORT kWelcome
   109              EXPORT kReject
   110              EXPORT kSay
   111              EXPORT kSaid
   112              EXPORT kLeave
   113              EXPORT kJoined
   114              EXPORT kLeft
   115              EXPORT kRename
   116              EXPORT kRenOk
   117              EXPORT kWho
   118              EXPORT kMap
   119              EXPORT kRooms
   120              EXPORT kTopic
   121              EXPORT kAllWho
   122              EXPORT kHist
   123              EXPORT kDirect
   124              EXPORT kDirGot
   125              EXPORT kDirSent
   126              EXPORT kDirBad
   127          
   128              % THE TRUNK KINDS ARE EXPORTED HERE, WITH THE REST, AND NOT BESIDE THEIR
   129              % OWN DECLARATIONS FURTHER DOWN - WHERE THEY READ BETTER AND DO NOT WORK.
   130              %
   131              % EXPORT obeys the same rule as IMPORT: it belongs in the block BEFORE any
   132              % ordinary declaration. Ten of them sat under their own comment after the
   133              % client kinds were declared, and every one drew
   134              %
   135              %     131  *** ERROR   - MISPLACED STATEMENT "EXPORT"
   136              %
   137              % MEASURED 2026-08-27, and the reason it survived a whole build cycle is
   138              % worth more than the fix: the linker reported NO undefined entries, the
   139              % test program ran, and all 139 checks PASSED - including the ones that
   140              % read kTrkRly back as 52. A compile with ten errors linked and ran and
   141              % said 'codec is good'. Only the LISTING said otherwise.
   142              EXPORT kTrkHello
   143              EXPORT kTrkWho
   144              EXPORT kTrkMbrs
   145              EXPORT kTrkSaid
   146              EXPORT kTrkRly
   147              EXPORT kTrkRlId
   148              EXPORT kTrkDir
   149              EXPORT kTrkDBad
   150              EXPORT trkAsk
   151              EXPORT trkAnswer
   152              EXPORT maxHops
   153          
   154              INTEGER : kJoin     := 1     % a client asking to come in
   155              INTEGER : kWelcome  := 2     % you are in, and this is your name
   156              INTEGER : kReject   := 3     % you are not, and this is why
   157              INTEGER : kSay      := 4     % a client speaking
   158              INTEGER : kSaid     := 5     % the room being told, SPEAKER INCLUDED
   159              INTEGER : kLeave    := 6     % a client going
   160              INTEGER : kJoined   := 7     % somebody arrived - everyone EXCEPT them
   161              INTEGER : kLeft     := 8     % somebody went - everyone EXCEPT them
   162              INTEGER : kRename   := 9     % change my nickname
   163              INTEGER : kRenOk    := 10    % it changed - everyone, ASKER INCLUDED
   164              INTEGER : kWho      := 11    % who is in this room
   165              INTEGER : kMap      := 12    % the machines and their trunks
   166              INTEGER : kRooms    := 13    % what rooms exist
   167              INTEGER : kTopic    := 14    % the room topic, asked or set
   168              INTEGER : kAllWho   := 15    % everybody, every room
   169              INTEGER : kHist     := 16    % a replayed line, MARKED as history
   170              INTEGER : kDirect   := 17    % a private message going out
   171              INTEGER : kDirGot   := 18    % a private message arriving
   172              INTEGER : kDirSent  := 19    % it reached them
   173              INTEGER : kDirBad   := 20    % it did not, and this is why
   174          
   175              % ---- THE TRUNK KINDS, SERVER TO SERVER ------------------------------
   176              %
   177              % 48 and up, left clear of the admin block so a future admin verb cannot
   178              % collide with a trunk one. Only the SERVER speaks these - a client never
   179              % sees one - so they live here for the FORMAT's sake rather than to remove
   180              % a duplicate declaration: the relay header below is the most intricate
   181              % framing in the product and it had no test of any kind.
   182              %
   183              % TWO NAMES CHANGED, and it is the seven-character rule again: kTrkRelay and
   184              % kTrkRelId BOTH read KTRKREL across a BRF boundary, so exporting both would
   185              % silently resolve every import to whichever the linker met first. They are
   186              % kTrkRly and kTrkRlId here - KTRKRLY and KTRKRLI, distinct. THE WIRE
   187              % NUMBERS, 52 AND 53, ARE UNCHANGED.
   188          
   189              % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the TEXT'S FIRST BYTE says which way: 0 is
   190              % "I am here, are you?" and 1 is "I am here, and I am answering you".
   191              % Without that byte two servers answering each other's HELLO would say hello
   192              % for ever. An ANSWER is never answered.
   193              INTEGER : kTrkHello := 48
   194              INTEGER : trkAsk    := 0
   195              INTEGER : trkAnswer := 1
   196          
   197              % "Tell me who is on you" and the answer. TWO kinds, unlike the hello: the
   198              % ask is empty and the answer is a list, so one kind would force a receiver
   199              % to judge by length.
   200              INTEGER : kTrkWho   := 49
   201              INTEGER : kTrkMbrs  := 50
   202          
   203              % Somebody spoke on another machine. NAME = the speaker as their own machine
   204              % knows them; TEXT = "ROOM/what they said". The RECEIVER adds the "@Dnnn",
   205              % because only it knows which system the letter came from - taken from the
   206              % magic, not from anything the sender claimed. A speaker cannot forge the
   207              % machine they are on. NOT relayed onward: that is kTrkRly.
   208              INTEGER : kTrkSaid  := 51
   209          
   210              % A line being RELAYED onward: kTrkSaid with THREE bytes in front - the
   211              % origin system big-endian, then how many hops it may still take.
   212              INTEGER : kTrkRly   := 52
   213          
   214              % A relayed line that also carries the number its ORIGIN gave it: two more
   215              % bytes after the hop count. THE ID MUST COME FROM THE ORIGIN - a machine
   216              % with two peers sends one line to both, and if each relay stamped its own
   217              % number a machine further out would see two names for one line and show it
   218              % twice, which is the very thing it exists to stop.
   219              INTEGER : kTrkRlId  := 53
   220          
   221              % A private message crossing a trunk. SAME six-byte header as kTrkRlId; what
   222              % differs is only what the text holds - TARGET/message rather than
   223              % ROOM/message.
   224              INTEGER : kTrkDir   := 54
   225          
   226              % A private message that could NOT be delivered at the far end, travelling
   227              % BACK to the machine the sender is on. SAME six-byte header again, but read
   228              % it the other way round: the origin is where the SENDER sits, which is what
   229              % routes this home, and the lineId is the one the FAILED message carried, so
   230              % the sender's own server can say which message it was.
   231              %
   232              % The text is TARGET/reason - the same shape kDirBad already uses locally,
   233              % which is the whole point: the receiving server hands it to the code it
   234              % already has and no client changes at all.
   235              %
   236              % Named kTrkDBad and not kTrkDirBad. Names collide at SEVEN characters across
   237              % a BRF, where kTrkDirBad would read KTRKDIR and be folded into kTrkDir by a
   238              % linker that does not call it a duplicate - it just resolves every import to
   239              % whichever it met first. kTrkDBa is clear of every other exported name.
   240              INTEGER : kTrkDBad  := 55
   241          
   242              % How far a line may travel from the machine it was typed on. The count is
   243              % what makes a loop terminate, not the topology.
   244              INTEGER : maxHops   := 3
   245          
   246              % The relay header's own routines are further down, AFTER the chat-frame
   247              % encoders they build on. PLANC is single-pass: a routine used above its
   248              % declaration draws ILLEGAL SYNTAX at the CALL, which reads like a
   249              % punctuation problem and sends you to the wrong line entirely.
   250          
   251              % ---- ENCODE, THE ONE THAT DOES THE WORK -----------------------------
   252              %
   253              % Writes a chat frame into buf STARTING AT dstAt, reading its text from src
   254              % starting at srcAt. Answers the position just past what it wrote.
   255              %
   256              % WHY A DESTINATION OFFSET EXISTS. A relayed line is a chat frame with a
   257              % relay header in front of it, so something has to write a frame that does
   258              % not start at zero. The obvious way is to hand the encoder a subarray -
   259              % cmEncAt(buf(hlen : bufMax - 1), ...) - and THAT IS THE ONE FORM THIS
   260              % PROJECT KNOWS IS BROKEN: a subarray with both bounds computed compiled
   261              % clean, ran, and wrote NOTHING AT ALL. An offset costs one addition and
   262              % cannot fail that way.
   263              %
   264              % cmEnc and cmEncAt are both this routine with the offsets fixed, so there
   265              % is exactly ONE piece of code that knows the frame layout.
   266              %
   267              % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS. Passing two subarrays of
   268              % one array to a single routine corrupts both, compiles clean, and has cost
   269              % this project a day.
   270              %
   271              % bufMax is the size of the WHOLE buffer, not what is left after dstAt.
   272              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, &
   273                      INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
   274                      cmBody(buf, dstAt, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
   275                  INTEGER : i
   276                  INTEGER : at
   277                  INTEGER : nl
   278                  INTEGER : tl
   279                  INTEGER : room
   280          
   281                  % What is actually available from dstAt onwards. Everything below is
   282                  % clamped against THIS, not against bufMax, or an offset frame would be
   283                  % allowed to run off the end by exactly dstAt bytes.
   284                  %
   285                  % THREE, not four: the kind byte is NOT part of the body.
   286                  bufMax - dstAt =: room
   287                  IF room < 3 THEN
   288                      dstAt RETURN
   289                  ENDIF
   290          
   291                  nameLen =: nl
   292                  IF nl < 0 THEN
   293                      0 =: nl
   294                  ENDIF
   295                  IF nl > 255 THEN
   296                      255 =: nl
   297                  ENDIF
   298          
   299                  % THE NAME MUST FIT TOO, and for a long time it did not - the clamp
   300                  % below reduced only the TEXT, so a 255-byte name wrote past the end
   301                  % while the text clamped to nothing. A name needs room for its own
   302                  % length byte and the 2-byte text length, so the most that fits is
   303                  % room - 3.
   304                  IF nl > room - 3 THEN
   305                      room - 3 =: nl
   306                  ENDIF
   307          
   308                  srcLen =: tl
   309                  IF tl < 0 THEN
   310                      0 =: tl
   311                  ENDIF
   312                  IF 3 + nl + tl > room THEN
   313                      room - 3 - nl =: tl
   314                      IF tl < 0 THEN
   315                          0 =: tl
   316                      ENDIF
   317                  ENDIF
   318          
   319                  nl =: buf(dstAt)
   320                  dstAt + 1 =: at
   321          
   322                  % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. A PLANC string literal starts at index 0, and
   323                  % writing name(i) inside a 1:n loop skips the first character and reads
   324                  % one byte past the end - which is how a nickname went out as ONNY
   325                  % instead of RONNY, with nothing reporting it.
   326                  IF nl > 0 THEN
   327                      FOR i IN 1:nl DO
   328                          name(i - 1) =: buf(at)
   329                          at + 1 =: at
   330                      ENDFOR
   331                  ENDIF
   332          
   333                  tl / 256 =: buf(at)
   334                  tl MOD 256 =: buf(at + 1)
   335                  at + 2 =: at
   336          
   337                  IF tl > 0 THEN
   338                      FOR i IN 1:tl DO
   339                          src(srcAt + i - 1) =: buf(at)
   340                          at + 1 =: at
   341                      ENDFOR
   342                  ENDIF
   343          
   344                  at RETURN
   345              ENDROUTINE
   346          
   347              % ---- A WHOLE FRAME: the kind byte, then the body --------------------
   348              %
   349              % Answers the position just past what it wrote.
   350              %
   351              % THE SPLIT BETWEEN THIS AND cmBody IS THE WIRE FORMAT, not tidiness. An
   352              % ordinary message is kind + body. A RELAYED one is kind + relay header +
   353              % body - THE KIND IS NOT REPEATED in front of the body, which is exactly
   354              % what the server's own kind-53 builder has always done:
   355              %
   356              %     kTrkRelId =: outBuf(0)          the kind
   357              %     ...origin, hops, id...          bytes 1..5
   358              %     memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(6)    the NAME LENGTH - no second kind byte
   359              %
   360              % MEASURED 2026-08-27: writing a whole frame after the relay header put a
   361              % kind byte where the name length belongs, and trStrip then answered 53 as
   362              % the name length. Four tests caught it in thirty seconds, which is the
   363              % entire argument for this file existing.
   364              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, &
   365                      INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
   366                      cmEncTo(buf, dstAt, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
   367                  IF bufMax - dstAt < 4 THEN
   368                      dstAt RETURN
   369                  ENDIF
   370                  kind =: buf(dstAt)
   371                  cmBody(buf, dstAt + 1, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) RETURN
   372              ENDROUTINE
   373          
   374              % ---- THE RELAY HEADER -----------------------------------------------
   375              %
   376              % Three shapes share one buffer, and telling them apart is the whole job:
   377              %
   378              %   any other kind     0 kind | 1 nameLen | 2.. name | len | text
   379              %   kTrkRly    (52)    0 kind | 1-2 origin | 3 hops              | BODY
   380              %   kTrkRlId   (53)    0 kind | 1-2 origin | 3 hops | 4-5 lineId | BODY
   381              %   kTrkDir    (54)    the same six as kTrkRlId
   382              %   kTrkDBad   (55)    the same six again - a refusal traveling home
   383              %
   384              % BODY is nameLen | name | text length | text - the frame WITHOUT its kind
   385              % byte, because the kind is already at byte 0 and is not repeated.
   386              %
   387              % The origin is BIG-ENDIAN, high byte first, like every other two-byte field
   388              % in this protocol.
   389              %
   390              % THIS WAS PARSED BY HAND IN TWO PLACES with the length tests written out
   391              % twice, and it is the most intricate framing the product has. A truncated
   392              % relay header read as a chat frame produces a PLAUSIBLE-LOOKING message out
   393              % of rubbish - the origin's high byte lands where the name length belongs -
   394              % which is why the tests below matter more than the ones on the chat frame.
   395          
   396              % How many bytes of relay header this kind carries. 0 for anything that is
   397              % not a relayed line, so a caller can ask about ANY kind and act on the
   398              % answer without a special case.
   399              % A HISTORY LINE IS NOT A RELAY, AND IT STILL HAS A HEADER. The seven
   400              % bytes are the ND calendar - see hsSec and friends below - and they sit
   401              % exactly where a relay header sits, so trStrip removes them with no new
   402              % code and the frame underneath is an ordinary chat message again.
   403              %
   404              % THE TWO CAN NEVER BOTH BE PRESENT. History is local to one machine and
   405              % deliberately does not cross a trunk, so no kind needs both headers and
   406              % this routine never has to add them together.
   407              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : trHLen(kind)
   408                  IF kind = kHist THEN
   409                      8 RETURN
   410                  ENDIF
   411                  IF kind = kTrkRly THEN
   412                      4 RETURN
   413                  ENDIF
   414                  IF kind = kTrkRlId THEN
   415                      6 RETURN
   416                  ENDIF
   417                  IF kind = kTrkDir THEN
   418                      6 RETURN
   419                  ENDIF
   420                  IF kind = kTrkDBad THEN
   421                      6 RETURN
   422                  ENDIF
   423                  0 RETURN
   424              ENDROUTINE
   425          
   426              % The machine the line was TYPED on - not the one that passed it along.
   427              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trOrig(buf)
   428                  buf(1) * 256 + buf(2) RETURN
   429              ENDROUTINE
   430          
   431              % How many more hops this line may take.
   432              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trHops(buf)
   433                  buf(3) RETURN
   434              ENDROUTINE
   435          
   436              % The number the ORIGIN gave this line. Only on kinds 53 and 54.
   437              %
   438              % A peer that sent a high byte over 127 overflows a signed 16-bit INTEGER
   439              % and lands here NEGATIVE. Ugly and harmless: this number is only ever
   440              % compared for equality, never used as a size or an index, so a negative one
   441              % still names the line perfectly well.
   442              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trId(buf)
   443                  buf(4) * 256 + buf(5) RETURN
   444              ENDROUTINE
   445          
   446              % ---- WHEN A REPLAYED LINE WAS SAID -----------------------------------
   447              %
   448              % Only on kHist, and only in the seven bytes trHLen accounts for. Read
   449              % them BEFORE trStrip, exactly like the relay fields: afterwards they are
   450              % gone and buf(1) is the name length again.
   451              %
   452              % THE ND'S OWN CALENDAR, kept as the machine gives it - no epoch, no text.
   453              % Second first and the year last, big-endian like every other two-byte
   454              % field here, which is also the order MN113 fills its array.
   455              %
   456              % A YEAR OF ZERO MEANS THE TIME WAS NEVER RECORDED - the line came out of
   457              % a history block written before there was anywhere to put one. Say so on
   458              % the screen; do not invent a moment. The year is the test and not the
   459              % whole value, because midnight exactly is a real time and must not read
   460              % as unknown.
   461              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsSec(buf)
   462                  buf(1) RETURN
   463              ENDROUTINE
   464          
   465              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsMin(buf)
   466                  buf(2) RETURN
   467              ENDROUTINE
   468          
   469              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsHour(buf)
   470                  buf(3) RETURN
   471              ENDROUTINE
   472          
   473              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsDay(buf)
   474                  buf(4) RETURN
   475              ENDROUTINE
   476          
   477              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsMon(buf)
   478                  buf(5) RETURN
   479              ENDROUTINE
   480          
   481              % The FULL year - 1998, not 98. Halving it into one byte was a real defect
   482              % once: a three-digit value indexed past the end of a ten-byte literal and
   483              % printed a blank where a digit belonged.
   484              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsYear(buf)
   485                  buf(6) * 256 + buf(7) RETURN
   486              ENDROUTINE
   487          
   488              % TAKE THE RELAY HEADER OFF, leaving an ordinary chat frame in place.
   489              %
   490              % Slides everything after the header down so that buf(1) is the name length
   491              % again, and answers the NEW length. The kind byte is left alone, so the
   492              % caller still knows what it was reading.
   493              %
   494              % Answers -1 if the message is too short to hold the header it claims -
   495              % which is the case that would otherwise build a whole message out of
   496              % rubbish. Read the origin, hops and id BEFORE calling this: they are gone
   497              % afterwards.
   498              %
   499              % A kind with no relay header answers the length unchanged and touches
   500              % nothing, so a receive loop can call it unconditionally.
   501              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : trStrip(buf, arrived, kind)
   502                  INTEGER : i
   503                  INTEGER : hlen
   504                  INTEGER : gone
   505          
   506                  trHLen(kind) =: hlen
   507                  IF hlen = 0 THEN
   508                      arrived RETURN
   509                  ENDIF
   510          
   511                  % STRICTLY GREATER, not "at least". A message exactly the length of its
   512                  % own header carries no chat frame at all, and sliding zero bytes would
   513                  % leave the old name length in place.
   514                  IF arrived <= hlen THEN
   515                      -1 RETURN
   516                  ENDIF
   517          
   518                  % The kind byte stays at 0, so what disappears is hlen - 1 bytes.
   519                  hlen - 1 =: gone
   520                  FOR i IN 1:arrived - hlen DO
   521                      buf(i + gone) =: buf(i)
   522                  ENDFOR
   523          
   524                  arrived - gone RETURN
   525              ENDROUTINE
   526          
   527              % PUT A RELAY HEADER ON, in front of a chat frame this routine also writes.
   528              %
   529              % Answers the total length, or -1 if the buffer cannot hold even the header
   530              % and an empty frame.
   531              %
   532              % lineId is ignored for kind 52, which has no room for it - passing one is
   533              % not an error, because a caller that relays both kinds should not have to
   534              % branch just to call this.
   535              %
   536              % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS, as for cmEnc.
   537              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, &
   538                      INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
   539                      trEnc(buf, kind, origin, hops, lineId, name, nameLen, &
   540                            src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
   541                  INTEGER : hlen
   542                  INTEGER : body
   543          
   544                  trHLen(kind) =: hlen
   545                  IF hlen = 0 THEN
   546                      -1 RETURN
   547                  ENDIF
   548                  IF bufMax < hlen + 4 THEN
   549                      -1 RETURN
   550                  ENDIF
   551          
   552                  kind =: buf(0)
   553                  origin / 256 =: buf(1)
   554                  origin MOD 256 =: buf(2)
   555                  hops =: buf(3)
   556                  IF hlen = 6 THEN
   557                      lineId / 256 =: buf(4)
   558                      lineId MOD 256 =: buf(5)
   559                  ENDIF
   560          
   561                  % THE BODY GOES STRAIGHT AFTER THE HEADER, with NO second kind byte -
   562                  % byte hlen is the name length. That is what the server has always put
   563                  % on the wire, and getting it wrong here produced a message whose name
   564                  % length read as 53.
   565                  %
   566                  % Written at an OFFSET rather than by handing an encoder a subarray:
   567                  % buf(hlen : bufMax - 1) has both bounds computed, and this project has
   568                  % a measured case of exactly that form compiling clean, running, and
   569                  % writing NOTHING AT ALL.
   570                  cmBody(buf, hlen, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) =: body
   571          
   572                  body RETURN
   573              ENDROUTINE
   574          
   575              % ---- ENCODE, from the start of a name and a text --------------------
   576              %
   577              % The ordinary case: a whole name and a whole text, both read from the
   578              % beginning of their arrays. Answers the total length written into buf.
   579              %
   580              % ONE LINE, because it is cmEncTo with BOTH offsets at zero. There is exactly
   581              % one routine in this product that knows the frame layout, which is the
   582              % entire argument for the library - three copies of it agreed only because
   583              % somebody kept checking.
   584              %
   585              % buf, name and text must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS - see cmEncTo.
   586              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, &
   587                      INTEGER) : cmEnc(buf, kind, name, nameLen, text, textLen, bufMax)
   588                  cmEncTo(buf, 0, kind, name, nameLen, text, 0, textLen, bufMax) RETURN
   589              ENDROUTINE
   590          
   591              % ---- ENCODE, WITH THE TEXT AT AN OFFSET -----------------------------
   592              %
   593              % The same message, but the text is read from `src` starting at `srcAt`
   594              % rather than from the beginning of an array. The server's text sits inside
   595              % its receive buffer at an offset, so this is the shape it needs.
   596              %
   597              % IT IS NOT A SUBARRAY, and that is the whole point. The obvious call would
   598              % be cmEnc(..., inBuf(textAt : textAt + textLength - 1), ...), which is a
   599              % subarray with BOTH bounds computed - and this project has one measured case
   600              % of exactly that form compiling clean, running, and WRITING NOTHING AT ALL.
   601              % An offset costs one addition and cannot fail that way.
   602              %
   603              % ONE LINE, because it is cmEncTo with the DESTINATION offset at zero. Every
   604              % rule about clamping, subscripting from zero and the two-byte length lives
   605              % there and is stated once.
   606              %
   607              % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS - see cmEncTo.
   608              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, &
   609                      INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
   610                      cmEncAt(buf, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
   611                  cmEncTo(buf, 0, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) RETURN
   612              ENDROUTINE
   613          
   614              % ---- DECODE ---------------------------------------------------------
   615              %
   616              % Four small readers rather than one routine handing back a record: PLANC
   617              % has no multiple return, and a record would have to be declared in both
   618              % programs - which is the duplication this library exists to remove.
   619          
   620              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmKind(buf)
   621                  buf(0) RETURN
   622              ENDROUTINE
   623          
   624              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmNmLen(buf)
   625                  buf(1) RETURN
   626              ENDROUTINE
   627          
   628              % Where the text starts. The name length decides it, so a corrupt length
   629              % moves the text - which is why every caller clamps before using this.
   630              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmTxAt(buf)
   631                  buf(1) + 4 RETURN
   632              ENDROUTINE
   633          
   634              % HIGH BYTE TIMES 256 PLUS LOW. Reading only the low byte would work for
   635              % every message this product has ever sent and would be wrong for the
   636              % first one that did not.
   637              %
   638              % IT READS TWO BYTES THE CALLER MAY NOT HAVE. The pair sits at
   639              % buf(1) + 2, and buf(1) is ONE BYTE off a wire - it can say 255, which
   640              % points at buf(257) in a 256-byte buffer. The callers' guard
   641              % (2 + nameLength + 1 > readLength) permits nameLength + 3 = readLength, so
   642              % a 256-byte message with a 253-byte name still reads one past the end.
   643              % Narrow, and a READ rather than a write, but it is exactly the kind of
   644              % thing that is impossible to see in PLANC.
   645              %
   646              % cmLenN is the safe form: it is told how many bytes ARRIVED and answers
   647              % -1 if the length field is not wholly inside them. cmTxLen is kept as it
   648              % was so no existing caller changes behaviour without being edited.
   649              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmTxLen(buf)
   650                  INTEGER : at
   651                  buf(1) + 2 =: at
   652                  buf(at) * 256 + buf(at + 1) RETURN
   653              ENDROUTINE
   654          
   655              % The same reading, bounded by what actually arrived.
   656              %
   657              % Answers -1 when the message is too short to hold the field, which is a
   658              % value no real length can take - so a caller that ignores it gets an
   659              % obviously wrong answer rather than a plausible one built out of whatever
   660              % the previous, longer message left in the buffer.
   661              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER) : cmLenN(buf, arrived)
   662                  INTEGER : at
   663          
   664                  % Two bytes for kind and name length must be there before buf(1) means
   665                  % anything at all. Nothing in either program checked even this.
   666                  IF arrived < 2 THEN
   667                      -1 RETURN
   668                  ENDIF
   669          
   670                  buf(1) + 2 =: at
   671          
   672                  % Both bytes of the pair must be inside the message: the last one is at
   673                  % at + 1, so the message needs at + 2 bytes.
   674                  IF at + 2 > arrived THEN
   675                      -1 RETURN
   676                  ENDIF
   677          
   678                  buf(at) * 256 + buf(at + 1) RETURN
   679              ENDROUTINE
   680          
   681              % Is this buffer a message at all? Answers FALSE for anything too short to
   682              % hold the fixed part of a header, or whose name and length fields do not
   683              % fit inside what arrived.
   684              %
   685              % WHY IT EXISTS: neither program checked that TWO bytes had arrived before
   686              % reading buf(0) and buf(1). A zero-length or one-byte delivery decoded
   687              % stale bytes left by the previous message and acted on them.
   688              ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (BYTES, INTEGER) : cmSane(buf, arrived)
   689                  IF arrived < 4 THEN
   690                      FALSE RETURN
   691                  ENDIF
   692                  IF buf(1) + 4 > arrived THEN
   693                      FALSE RETURN
   694                  ENDIF
   695                  TRUE RETURN
   696              ENDROUTINE
   697          
   698              % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
   699              % HOW MANY BYTES IS IT SAFE TO READ INTO THIS BUFFER?
   700              %
   701              % want is the SENDER'S message size, handed back by XMSG - never a size
   702              % this program chose. PLANC checks no array bounds, so handing it straight
   703              % to XMPFREA lets whoever is at the other end write past the end of the
   704              % buffer. That happens BEFORE a single field is parsed, so none of the
   705              % careful field checks in this library can save it.
   706              %
   707              % BOTH PROGRAMS DID THIS BY HAND, and both compared against a hand-kept
   708              % constant sitting beside the declaration:
   709              %
   710              %     INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
   711              %     BYTES : inBuf(0:255)
   712              %
   713              % Two facts that have to agree, written in two places, checked by nobody.
   714              % Widen the array and forget the constant and the clamp quietly keeps the
   715              % old smaller value; SHRINK the array and forget it and the clamp lets a
   716              % write past the end. MAXINDEX asks the array itself, so the size cannot
   717              % drift from the declaration - the same fix as the eighteen hand-counted
   718              % string lengths in TESTUI.
   719              %
   720              % AND IT CLAMPS THE LOW END, WHICH NEITHER PROGRAM DID. I have not measured
   721              % XMSG returning a negative length and I am not claiming it can. What is in
   722              % the guide is that on a RETURNED message (XMTRE) the length field carries
   723              % an error status instead of a length - so the field is not always a length
   724              % at all, and CHATSV keeps its own copy in receiveStat for exactly that
   725              % reason. One compare removes the question rather than reasoning about it.
   726              % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
   727              ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, BYTES) : xrClamp(want, buf)
   728                  INTEGER : room
   729          
   730                  % The array's own declared size, asked of the array. MAXINDEX gives the
   731                  % upper BOUND, so a (0:255) buffer answers 255 and holds 256 bytes.
   732                  MAXINDEX(buf, 1) + 1 =: room
   733          
   734                  IF want < 0 THEN
   735                      0 RETURN
   736                  ENDIF
   737                  IF want > room THEN
   738                      room RETURN
   739                  ENDIF
   740                  want RETURN
   741              ENDROUTINE
   742          
   743              % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
   744              % THE ADDRESS OF A BUFFER THIS ROUTINE WAS HANDED.
   745              %
   746              % This exists to ANSWER A QUESTION, and the answer decides whether the XMSG
   747              % transport can move into a library at all.
   748              %
   749              % Every ADDR in this product is taken of a MODULE-LEVEL array - inBuf,
   750              % outBuf, letterBuf - because every XMSG call sits in the same module as its
   751              % buffer:
   752              %
   753              %     xmpfrea(0, 0, ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, 0, want, got)
   754              %
   755              % A shared transport routine cannot do that. It is HANDED the buffer, so it
   756              % must take the address of a PARAMETER, and NOTHING IN THIS PRODUCT HAS EVER
   757              % DONE THAT. PLANC passes arrays by reference, so it should give the caller's
   758              % own array - but "should" is how a wrong address gets written to, and a
   759              % wrong address handed to XMPFREA is a write to memory nobody owns. PLANC
   760              % checks no array bounds and would report nothing at all.
   761              %
   762              % So it is measured rather than assumed, by a test that needs no XMSG: ask
   763              % this routine for the address, take ADDR of the same array at the call site,
   764              % and compare. Equal means a parameter address is the caller's array and the
   765              % transport can safely be shared. See CHATTST.
   766              %
   767              % xrAddr rather than XMUSERADDRESS: that type lives in the XMSG include, which
   768              % this library deliberately does not pull in. It is the SAME WIDTH - 16-bit
   769              % INTEGER - and the width is the part that matters; see the note above the
   770              % MODULE for what a 32-bit force costs.
   771              % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
   772              ROUTINE VOID, xrAddr (BYTES) : xrAddrOf(buf)
   773                  ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE xrAddr RETURN
   774              ENDROUTINE
   775          
   776          ENDMODULE
   777          